From: Jay Leaton <58495321+jayleaton@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 18:37:57 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 62/72] deepseek: fold wo_a's input rotation into the attention
 split-merge (TF_DSV41_ROT_FOLD bit 1)

With TF_DSV41_ROT_FOLD bit 1 set, the attention split-merge also writes
wo_a's rotated fp16 input rows: each 128-column block of the merged output
is rounded as the bf16 store rounds it, widened as rot_in reads it, then
scaled by wo_a's suh, transformed and stored exactly as seg_rot_in_kernel
does. wo_a's rot_in launch is dropped (one per layer per rank and round);
L.o is still written. A layer keeps H's path unless every wo_a group starts
and ends on the 128-column grid. Bit 2 (q-norm) stays reserved and refused.

The fold runs new split_rot instances beside the existing split kernel;
with bit 1 clear the emitted windows are unchanged. Host tests cover the
knob, the emitted windows, and a bitwise twin of the rotation path. Each
first-stage product is a bf16 times an f16 and so exact in fp32, which
keeps the twin exact whether or not the compiler contracts it.
`tf-dsv41-test rot-fold-attn` compares the bytes on a GPU.

Mechanism after Bertholomus's dsv41 attention-merge RotOut fold
(tri_attn.zig, round.zig), re-implemented here; credited in docs/credits.md.

Co-authored-by: bertholomus <311723197+bertholomus@users.noreply.github.com>
